652,"type":"genre","name":"Noise Rock","n":3},{"id":255263121494129,"type":"genre","name":"Metal","n":2},{"id":237890000566646,"type":"genre","name":"Post-Hardcore","n":2},{"id":185670346256268,"type":"genre","name":"rock","n":2},{"id":31482837869539,"type":"genre","name":"Punk","n":2},{"id":210192360016800,"type":"genre","name":"Alternative Rock","n":1}],"labels":[],"album_labels":[],"own_scenes":[],"scenes":[{"id":8186300681761,"name":"Más ruidoso que el rock: noise-rock de los noventa","book":"A History of Rock Music 1951-2000","author":"Piero Scaruffi","start":1989,"end":1998,"desc":"En los noventa los géneros de los ochenta (lo-fi, industrial, gótico, roots-rock, noise-rock, indie-pop, techno, ambient) se separan en lugar de fundirse: es la revancha de «la provincia» (el Sur, Seattle) y la década con menos géneros nuevos (grunge, post-rock, trip-hop, drum'n'bass, glitch). La influencia de Sonic Youth y Pussy Galore es la más visible: Bewitched, Boss Hog, Jon Spencer Blues Explosion (deconstrucción posmoderna del blues), Versus. El hardcore apocalíptico de Chicago: Jesus Lizard (Goat), Dazzling Killmen, Unsane. Polvo y la escuela de Chapel Hill, Come, Supreme Dicks, Tsunami, Brainiac; en Inglaterra, Gallon Drunk, Jacob's Mouse, Prolapse; en Canadá, la escuela de Halifax (Eric's Trip, Sloan, Jale).","color":"#fb7185","family":"Punk, new wave, hardcore y noise","link":"https://volteret4.github.io/scaruffi/#noise-90s"}],"book_tracks":[{"id":242600150363991,"artist":"Unsane","artist_id":222079475548535,"title":"Unsane","year":1991,"ess":1,"yt":"S6TdhDFAFMw","kind":"a","book":{"id":8,"title":"A History of Rock Music 1951-2000","author":"Piero Scaruffi","url":"https://volteret4.github.io/scaruffi/"},"scene":"Más ruidoso que el rock: noise-rock de los noventa","scene_id":8186300681761,"scene_link":"https://volteret4.github.io/scaruffi/#noise-90s","chapters":["Sección 6.1"],"pages":[[1,227,null]]},{"id":60825900377583,"artist":"Unsane","artist_id":222079475548535,"title":"Total Destruction","year":1994,"ess":0,"yt":"FLwedcdxBuA","kind":"a","book":{"id":8,"title":"A History of Rock Music 1951-2000","author":"Piero Scaruffi","url":"https://volteret4.github.io/scaruffi/"},"scene":"Más ruidoso que el rock: noise-rock de los noventa","scene_id":8186300681761,"scene_link":"https://volteret4.github.io/scaruffi/#noise-90s","chapters":["Sección 6.1"],"pages":[[1,227,null]]}],"mentions":[{"book":"A History of Rock Music 1951-2000","author":"Piero Scaruffi","page":141,"text":"(1995), which features the super-group of guitarist Marc Ribot, drummer Vincent Signorelli (Unsane) and bassist Tod Ashley (Cop Shoot Cop), completed the metamorphosis presenting a gargantuan essay in the sampling and manipulation of musical…"},{"book":"A History of Rock Music 1951-2000","author":"Piero Scaruffi","page":226,"text":"…Cristina Martinez led Boss Hog (2), that re-invented party-music first on Cold Hands (1990), featuring Honeymoon Killers' bassist Jerry Teel and Unsane drummer Charlie Ondras, and then on White Out (2000), both clever revisitations of rock stereotypes. Like Pussy Galore, the Jon Spencer Blues…"},{"book":"A History of Rock Music 1951-2000","author":"Piero Scaruffi","page":227,"text":"…of Dig Out The Switch (1992). The band relished horror psychodramas of ferocious intensity, an art that culminated on Face Of Collapse (1994). Unsane (11), formed in New York by Jon's brother Chris Spencer and drummer Charlie Ondras, concocted a dissonant and violent form of rock'n'roll that borrowed the sheer impetus of hardcore but emptied it of any emotion and melody. The catastrophic riffs, hammering rhythms and uncontrolled vocals of Unsane (1991) performed glacial and relentless surgery on the body of a zombie. Cascades of atrocious sounds destabilized its songs and generated a form of…"},{"book":"A History of Rock Music 1951-2000","author":"Piero Scaruffi","page":301,"text":"…Calexico: The Black Light (Touch And Go) 75. Red House Painters: Down Colorful H